1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,500
The Black Hills of South Dakota, their ageless majesty has attracted pioneers and poets, artists and opportunists.

2
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:50,080
And in the early part of this century, they also inspired an idea so preposterous and patriotic that it captured the imagination of the world.

3
00:01:23,963 --> 00:01:27,280
Mount Rushmore, one of the world's greatest modern marvels.

4
00:01:27,281 --> 00:01:32,620
An astounding monument to the visionaries of a young nation.

5
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,940
An attempt to forge a new national identity.

6
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:40,160
A tribute to democracy carved out of solid granite

7
00:01:40,161 --> 00:01:44,800
by a man as energetic and contentious as the country he loved.

8
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:05,380
After four long years of fighting, World War I ended.

9
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:09,560
In the 1920s, with the return of American troops,

10
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:13,140
came a national exuberance, a joyous jazz spirit

11
00:02:13,141 --> 00:02:16,960
that seemed to encourage expansive, uninhibited behavior.

12
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:20,100
After all, we had won a war.

13
00:02:20,101 --> 00:02:21,360
We could do anything.

14
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:23,680
It was time to celebrate.

15
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,120
We embraced great heroes.

16
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:28,800
and great hooligans.

17
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:31,940
There were those who would legislate morality

18
00:02:31,941 --> 00:02:34,980
and those who would throw caution to the wind

19
00:02:34,981 --> 00:02:36,740
to reach dizzying new heights.

20
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:39,980
No one could predict the Great Depression

21
00:02:39,981 --> 00:02:42,560
that was only a few years away.

22
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:46,800
American industry was retooling from a war economy

23
00:02:46,801 --> 00:02:49,400
to accommodate a rapidly growing consumer market.

24
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,900
Mass production made new products available and affordable,

25
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:54,620
particularly automobiles.

26
00:02:54,621 --> 00:02:59,000
Henry Ford was grinding them out at a rate of nearly 22,000 a year.

27
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:02,680
The new American middle class was riding to work

28
00:03:02,681 --> 00:03:07,700
and turning the traditional family vacation into a great adventure on the road.

29
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:12,340
The money-making potential of this was not lost on Doan Robinson,

30
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:16,300
67-year-old state historian and poet laureate of South Dakota.

31
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,340
He longed to introduce people to the beauty of his state.

32
00:03:20,341 --> 00:03:24,880
But how do you convince tourists to drive for days on primitive highways

33
00:03:24,881 --> 00:03:28,700
to visit a forsaken corner of a largely undeveloped wilderness?

34
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:33,820
Robinson claimed he had an illuminating flash, an inspiration,

35
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:38,140
to distinguish South Dakota with a roadside attraction so unique

36
00:03:38,141 --> 00:03:41,200
that tourists would be compelled to visit.

37
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:46,140
He thought what would be great is if they'd carved some Western folk heroes,

38
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:49,940
Chief Red Cloud, Kit Carson, other folk heroes.

39
00:03:49,941 --> 00:03:52,660
If they could carve those in the Black Hills in the Needles District,

40
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:55,520
that tourists would come for miles to see them.

41
00:03:56,260 --> 00:04:00,880
The Needles are ancient stone structures, the eroded stubs of granite mountains.

42
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:03,900
There are literally thousands of them in the Black Hills.

43
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:07,700
They are rough, uneven, and impossible to carve.

44
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:12,320
But that was not enough to deter Doan Robinson.

45
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:16,680
All he really needed, he felt, was an artist who loved a challenge.

46
00:04:16,681 --> 00:04:21,680
That man was Gutzon Borglum, a renowned stone sculptor.

47
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:25,680
Gutzon was always looking for new worlds to conquer.

48
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:31,680
Gutzon believed that he was the best artist who has ever existed.

49
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:35,680
Borglum enjoyed working on an exaggerated scale.

50
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:40,680
He once lamented there was no monument in America as big as the nation's spirit.

51
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:43,680
He had done big work.

52
00:04:43,681 --> 00:04:48,880
work he had designed a mountain carving in 1915 at a Georgia site called Stone

53
00:04:48,881 --> 00:04:55,900
Mountain now South Dakota offered Borglum a new challenge the Rapid City

54
00:04:55,901 --> 00:05:00,920
Commercial Club had backed Robinson's concept basically said to Borglum hey

55
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:07,660
find a mountain pick a subject go to work we want a mark a mountain carving in

56
00:05:07,661 --> 00:05:13,260
1925 with the frontier guide and his son Lincoln Borglum explored the Black Hills

57
00:05:13,261 --> 00:05:16,120
for a carving site that would suggest a theme

58
00:05:16,121 --> 00:05:19,040
big enough for his bombastic reputation.

59
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:21,840
He found both at Mount Rushmore,

60
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:24,760
where he envisioned the ultimate American monument.

61
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:28,260
Unlike the needles, Mount Rushmore was solid granite.

62
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:31,560
Even though the rock was riddled with minute cracks,

63
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:34,380
Borglum became fixated on the site.

64
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,020
Here is the place, he declared,

65
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:40,680
American history shall march along that skyline.

66
00:05:40,681 --> 00:05:45,680
He and his men quickly scaled the peak and claimed the site as a national monument.

67
00:05:45,681 --> 00:05:49,680
He had no authority to do that, but that didn't stop him.

68
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:57,680
It was here Gutzon Borglum would labor for the next 16 years.

69
00:05:57,681 --> 00:06:02,680
Before he would die trying to make this dream come true, he would revise his designs nine times,

70
00:06:02,681 --> 00:06:09,680
remove a half a million tons of rock, and provoke some of the wildest political battles of the age.

71
00:06:09,681 --> 00:06:23,681
Like the America he loved, Gutsen Borglum

72
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:27,760
came from hearty immigrant stock, Danish Mormons

73
00:06:27,860 --> 00:06:30,740
seeking the new Jerusalem in the barren wastes of Utah.

74
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:37,740
Much of his early work glorified the female form,

75
00:06:37,741 --> 00:06:41,640
his female Atlas, and Conception.

76
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:46,320
As his work brought him recognition,

77
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:49,620
he moved into the world of the rich and powerful

78
00:06:49,621 --> 00:06:53,360
and carefully cultivated extraordinary friends.

79
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:59,460
The Wright brothers, he served as a timer on their early flights.

80
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,980
He campaigned for his friend Teddy Roosevelt's Bull Moose Party.

81
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:06,900
He wrote social and political tracts that provoked outrage,

82
00:07:06,901 --> 00:07:10,300
satisfying his appetite for confrontation.

83
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:13,700
And in 1923, he even worked on a national committee

84
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:18,440
trying to elect a Ku Klux Klan Grand Dragon to the White House.

85
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:21,140
He got into the Ku Klux Klan because he saw it

86
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:22,740
as a grassroots movement.

87
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:25,460
They were a national organization.

88
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:29,160
They had hundreds of thousands of members.

89
00:07:29,260 --> 00:07:31,800
And so for him, he was not really

90
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:35,200
part of some kind of lunatic fringe group.

91
00:07:35,201 --> 00:07:39,980
He really thought that the Klan could be worked into an organization

92
00:07:39,981 --> 00:07:45,760
that would elect a president without the power bosses.

93
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:49,100
Borglum's crusades reflected his approach to art.

94
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:53,720
He insisted on working in monumental scale and on mythic characters.

95
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:58,160
You were separated in those days by whether you did or did not do a Lincoln.

96
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:02,980
All of the Lincoln artists were people on another level.

97
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:04,080
It was the big leagues.

98
00:08:04,081 --> 00:08:07,780
He named his son Lincoln so he could write to committees and say,

99
00:08:07,781 --> 00:08:09,420
remember, Lincoln is one of mine.

100
00:08:09,421 --> 00:08:13,580
And he, of course, has done three of the best Lincolns,

101
00:08:13,581 --> 00:08:16,020
acknowledged the best Lincolns in existence.

102
00:08:16,021 --> 00:08:20,220
The one on Mount Rushmore, the seated Lincoln in Newark, New Jersey,

103
00:08:20,221 --> 00:08:23,540
and the Lincoln portrait that's in the Capitol building

104
00:08:23,541 --> 00:08:25,540
and on the grave in Springfield.

105
00:08:25,541 --> 00:08:30,240
It was ironic then that his biggest commission was the one in 1915,

106
00:08:30,241 --> 00:08:36,180
the attempt to make the South rise again through a mammoth tribute to the Confederacy, Stone Mountain.

107
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:42,660
Commissioned by the daughters of the Confederacy, the work was designed to be as tall as a 20-story building.

108
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:45,360
But the project soon fell apart.

109
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:49,800
In typical fashion, Borglum had a volatile disagreement with his patrons,

110
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:55,520
who believed the artist was insulting the memory of General Lee by portraying him wearing a hat.

111
00:08:56,360 --> 00:09:00,720
In a fitful rage, he smashed his working models and fled Georgia

112
00:09:00,721 --> 00:09:03,200
with a lynch mob snapping at his heels.

113
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:07,860
Borglum needed another project and fast to restore his reputation.

114
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:12,520
His prayers were answered in 1925 when he received Doan Robinson's notes

115
00:09:12,521 --> 00:09:15,180
suggesting the massive work in South Dakota.

116
00:09:15,181 --> 00:09:20,880
The backers of Mount Rushmore were able to say to Borglum time and time again,

117
00:09:20,881 --> 00:09:23,900
remember, Gudson, you can lose one mountain.

118
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:27,880
You can't lose two without being historically a fool.

119
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,320
You must prove yourself here.

120
00:09:30,980 --> 00:09:32,140
And he understood that.

121
00:09:33,220 --> 00:09:36,460
A new mountain, a new dream were what Borglum needed,

122
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:38,420
and Mount Rushmore offered both.

123
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:43,420
He quickly dismissed Robinson's suggestion of Western heroes in stone

124
00:09:43,421 --> 00:09:46,240
and instead envisioned a tribute to American presidents

125
00:09:46,241 --> 00:09:49,300
who symbolized the evolution of the American spirit,

126
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:53,740
Washington, father of the country who led the fight for American liberty.

127
00:09:54,840 --> 00:09:57,280
Jefferson, author of the Declaration of Independence,

128
00:09:57,880 --> 00:10:00,860
whose vision of a growing nation inspired the Louisiana Purchase.

129
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,820
Lincoln, the great emancipator who preserved the union of states at its darkest hour.

130
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:12,700
And Teddy Roosevelt, the spirited individualist who defined a vibrant 20th century America

131
00:10:12,701 --> 00:10:14,960
and was an old friend of Borglum's.

132
00:10:14,961 --> 00:10:19,760
It would be two years before Borglum would carve this dream in granite.

133
00:10:19,761 --> 00:10:23,560
First he needed money and a president to go along with the plan.

134
00:10:23,561 --> 00:10:36,280
America's Jazz Age fell under the leadership of a president whose deadpan expression and

135
00:10:36,281 --> 00:10:42,920
flat personality were a far cry from speakeasies and flappers.

136
00:10:42,921 --> 00:10:46,920
Alvin Coolidge, a man not known for a wild sense of humor.

137
00:10:47,560 --> 00:10:53,280
But in 1927, in an act of providence, he decided to vacation in South Dakota.

138
00:10:54,220 --> 00:11:00,180
Here was an opportunity Borglum hoped to secure official federal recognition for his dream of Mount Rushmore,

139
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:04,640
and maybe raise desperately needed funds to finally get the project underway.

140
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:11,680
To impress the president, South Dakota Governor William Buleau christened a hunting lodge the Summer White House.

141
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:15,500
A local stream was renamed Grace Coolidge Creek,

142
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:18,180
and it was stocked with prize-winning trout and netted

143
00:11:18,181 --> 00:11:21,020
so the fish couldn't swim far from the presidential hook.

144
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:26,040
He hunted around and found some old Scandinavian with a big nose

145
00:11:26,041 --> 00:11:32,320
and employed him to sit on the bank of the Grace Coolidge Creek

146
00:11:32,321 --> 00:11:36,540
so that the fish would be accustomed to seeing a big-nosed person there

147
00:11:36,541 --> 00:11:40,060
and would be scared away when the Coolidge arrived.

148
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:42,840
Well, the effort paid off.

149
00:11:43,460 --> 00:11:44,760
Coolidge began to loosen up.

150
00:11:45,380 --> 00:11:48,040
He began to wear hats, all kind of hats.

151
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:52,260
And Borglum was able to infect the president with Mount Rushmore fever.

152
00:11:54,220 --> 00:12:00,980
On August 10, 1927, over 1,000 people showed up to catch a glimpse of Calvin Coolidge

153
00:12:00,981 --> 00:12:06,560
as he and the Secret Service arrived on horseback to mark the start of work on Mount Rushmore.

154
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:09,760
At the ceremony, Coolidge declared,

155
00:12:09,761 --> 00:12:13,780
we have come here to dedicate a cornerstone laid by the hand of the Almighty.

156
00:12:14,520 --> 00:12:18,220
And he handed Gutz and Borglum six ceremonial drill bits.

157
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:20,900
Not to be upstaged by the president,

158
00:12:21,300 --> 00:12:24,080
Borglum immediately scaled the 6,000-foot peak

159
00:12:24,081 --> 00:12:28,280
and had himself lowered over the heads of the crowd to begin carving,

160
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:32,380
even though he hadn't actually worked out the entire design yet.

161
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:36,760
In Washington, as one of his last acts as president in 1929,

162
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:39,520
Coolidge signed the Rushmore Bill.

163
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:43,700
establishing matching federal funds for private donations to the monument.

164
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:48,560
To some critics, it was either the most visionary act of his presidency

165
00:12:48,561 --> 00:12:55,460
or a comic coda to the decade of decadence that preceded America's plummet into the Depression.

166
00:13:02,260 --> 00:13:08,120
Borglum's initial design featured only three figures, each rendered to mid-torso.

167
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:11,440
His style was obsessively representational,

168
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:14,580
relying on portraits, models, and life masks

169
00:13:14,581 --> 00:13:17,300
to capture every nuance of his subjects.

170
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:21,100
He began his designs as rough miniatures,

171
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:24,560
testing how they would respond to sunlight and shadow,

172
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:28,280
then reworking those into 30-foot-tall structures,

173
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:33,200
which still were only one-twelfth of what the finished work would be.

174
00:13:33,201 --> 00:13:35,200
The model is made out of plaster,

175
00:13:35,201 --> 00:13:38,040
and it also is made out of Excelsior,

176
00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:40,100
which was a packing material at that time.

177
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:44,740
It has nails, it has pine needles, pine cones.

178
00:13:44,960 --> 00:13:46,520
It was made from just about anything

179
00:13:46,521 --> 00:13:48,680
that Borglum had on stock at the time.

180
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:52,780
Borglum designed the eyes of his subjects

181
00:13:52,781 --> 00:13:54,920
to feature raised areas in the pupils,

182
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:56,860
suggesting a glint of light.

183
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:00,340
In Lincoln's eye, this required a 30-inch shaft of granite.

184
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:04,580
And he developed an almost impressionistic style

185
00:14:04,581 --> 00:14:07,720
to render the spectacles that framed Roosevelt's face.

186
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:13,860
In early films, Borglum describes the scope of the monument to Don Robinson.

187
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:19,040
And that is 60 feet in height, from the wig to the chin.

188
00:14:19,980 --> 00:14:24,360
Borglum how large in the pupil of that eye is about 3 feet in diameter.

189
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:27,560
The upper lid is 18 inches.

190
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:30,440
The head, as I told you, is 60 feet in height.

191
00:14:31,080 --> 00:14:32,780
The forehead is 20 feet in height.

192
00:14:32,781 --> 00:14:39,780
The nose is 20 feet in height and from the nose to the end of the cheek is another 20 feet.

193
00:14:39,781 --> 00:14:45,780
Even during the early stages the effect was overwhelming.

194
00:14:45,781 --> 00:14:52,780
People don't realize that this head and the first waist part is as tall as the Niagara Falls.

195
00:14:52,781 --> 00:14:58,780
And that Washington might sit in the Niagara Falls and the water would only break around his collar.

196
00:14:58,781 --> 00:15:02,580
Washington's head alone is as large as the Sphinx in Egypt.

197
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:06,340
The carving size dwarfs almost all ancient wonders.

198
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:12,620
To transfer the designs onto stone, Borglum used an ancient technique called pointing.

199
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:17,680
A circular plate measured in degrees is placed on top of the model.

200
00:15:18,420 --> 00:15:21,140
The degree which a boom points is recorded,

201
00:15:21,580 --> 00:15:24,680
as well as the length of the boom out from the center of the plate

202
00:15:24,681 --> 00:15:28,080
and how far down from the boom a string extends

203
00:15:28,081 --> 00:15:30,380
until it reaches an element of the design.

204
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:33,960
These three measurements are multiplied by 12

205
00:15:33,961 --> 00:15:37,900
and then duplicated on a larger pointing system on the mountain.

206
00:15:38,300 --> 00:15:40,200
This was the model that was used.

207
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:42,820
It was a 1 to 1 12 scale.

208
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:44,920
If you look onto the model itself,

209
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:48,300
there's a boom that comes out and then a plumb bob.

210
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:52,820
So one inch on the model would represent 12 inches on the mountain itself.

211
00:15:52,821 --> 00:15:59,080
Because of the high degree of accuracy needed, the pointer was the most important person on the mountain.

212
00:16:00,080 --> 00:16:03,460
In 1932, Borglum assigned his son, Lincoln, to that job.

213
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:11,400
And from his vantage point, it was Lincoln who first recognized that the monument was quickly becoming more than just carving in stone.

214
00:16:22,820 --> 00:16:36,820
It came to me in an almost terrifying manner, Borglum admitted, that I had never sensed

215
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:42,240
what I was planning.

216
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:48,340
Its dimensions, the vastness that lay here demanded the complete remodeling of the grouping

217
00:16:48,341 --> 00:16:49,240
I had been dreaming.

218
00:16:49,241 --> 00:16:57,140
As work proceeded, Borglum constantly had to change his design due to inconsistencies in the granite.

219
00:16:57,141 --> 00:17:05,540
His first attempt at a Jefferson head on Washington's right side had to be blasted away and a new one built on Washington's left.

220
00:17:05,541 --> 00:17:08,140
Nature was participating in the design.

221
00:17:08,141 --> 00:17:15,340
When you look at Mount Rushmore, you'll notice that the Jefferson head is tilted back or seems to be looking up at a higher angle than the rest of the heads.

222
00:17:15,341 --> 00:17:19,840
The romantic view of it is that Borglum saw Jefferson as a visionary,

223
00:17:19,841 --> 00:17:23,440
the person who saw where the country should go and knew how to take us there.

224
00:17:23,441 --> 00:17:27,020
Reality was is that there was a crack that ran through Jefferson's nose.

225
00:17:27,021 --> 00:17:30,280
They had to keep tilting it back until the crack no longer ran through the nose.

226
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:39,500
Borglum needed a specially constructed aerial tram to survey the work as it progressed

227
00:17:39,501 --> 00:17:42,700
and instruct the crew on how to approach refinements.

228
00:17:42,701 --> 00:17:46,240
We would see Borglum come up in the bucket,

229
00:17:46,241 --> 00:17:49,340
and he'd stop the bucket out there in front

230
00:17:49,341 --> 00:17:51,740
with the wind kind of moving him around,

231
00:17:51,741 --> 00:17:54,240
and he'd look at either Lincoln

232
00:17:54,241 --> 00:17:57,640
or whichever one he had men working on.

233
00:17:57,641 --> 00:18:00,780
He'd just look at it, and then he'd go up and get some paint

234
00:18:00,781 --> 00:18:04,320
and a brush and go down and paint what he wanted,

235
00:18:04,321 --> 00:18:07,920
nothing from here to three inches down to nothing.

236
00:18:07,921 --> 00:18:11,220
But then how he could make it come out looking like Lincoln

237
00:18:11,221 --> 00:18:15,720
like Jefferson is amazing to me, because it was so large

238
00:18:15,721 --> 00:18:20,020
when you got next to it, you just couldn't imagine it.

239
00:18:20,021 --> 00:18:23,160
It was an incredible challenge to envision the monument

240
00:18:23,161 --> 00:18:25,460
from such a close perspective.

241
00:18:25,461 --> 00:18:28,280
But Borglum knew the figures were there.

242
00:18:28,281 --> 00:18:32,180
It was just a matter of removing half a million tons of granite

243
00:18:32,181 --> 00:18:34,680
to reveal them.

244
00:18:34,681 --> 00:18:36,780
From the height of a 50-story building,

245
00:18:36,781 --> 00:18:39,820
workmen were carefully lowered by iron winches

246
00:18:39,821 --> 00:18:41,640
in special bosun chairs.

247
00:18:42,700 --> 00:18:45,080
Once the pointer verified a position,

248
00:18:45,081 --> 00:18:47,240
a workman used a pneumatic drill

249
00:18:47,241 --> 00:18:49,600
to create small holes for the dynamite,

250
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:52,500
for just enough dynamite

251
00:18:52,501 --> 00:18:56,180
to remove extra rock

252
00:18:56,181 --> 00:18:58,540
to within three inches of the finished surface,

253
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,920
yet not enough to damage the area to be hand-carved.

254
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,920
In all, it was an exacting process

255
00:19:07,921 --> 00:19:13,940
suggested to Borglum by European road builders who used the technique to blast holes in mountains

256
00:19:13,941 --> 00:19:16,160
to build tunnels through the Alps.

257
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:21,440
The more delicate work was called honeycombing the rock.

258
00:19:22,060 --> 00:19:25,700
The surface of the stone was textured with a close formation of drill holes,

259
00:19:26,180 --> 00:19:31,720
then smaller pieces of granite would be manually chipped off with a tool called a channel iron.

260
00:19:32,580 --> 00:19:33,940
That's a channel iron.

261
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:37,560
They use it in the regular jackhammer instead of a regular drill.

262
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:44,160
and then they cut between the holes so that you can wedge out pieces of rock without using powder.

263
00:19:45,260 --> 00:19:49,720
This channel iron, like the wide variety of steel drill bits used to shape the stone,

264
00:19:49,880 --> 00:19:52,000
was attached to a 60-pound jackhammer.

265
00:19:52,720 --> 00:19:58,280
It operated on compressed air piped up from the base of the mountain, 1,300 feet below.

266
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:03,620
And then the air would be funneled down into the jackhammer, which would make it go in and out,

267
00:20:03,621 --> 00:20:07,620
And then it would also, if I can lift it up here,

268
00:20:07,621 --> 00:20:11,620
the end has a hole in it which would allow the air to come

269
00:20:11,621 --> 00:20:15,620
through and then push the excess dust away. But when you were

270
00:20:15,621 --> 00:20:19,620
in a bosom chair with about a 40 mile an hour wind

271
00:20:19,621 --> 00:20:23,620
and hanging on a cable off the side of the mountain, and it might be 20 degrees

272
00:20:23,621 --> 00:20:27,620
out, you had to hold that jackhammer away from your body.

273
00:20:27,621 --> 00:20:31,620
And as they held it away, it would of course wear them out.

274
00:20:31,621 --> 00:20:34,620
There's a lot of crooked drill holes up there.

275
00:20:34,621 --> 00:20:38,620
Donald blames me for that.

276
00:20:38,621 --> 00:20:45,620
He couldn't drill straight.

277
00:20:45,621 --> 00:20:49,620
Anytime you drill a crooked hole, you've got to cut it out with a channel iron.

278
00:20:49,621 --> 00:20:51,620
You don't get out of working.

279
00:20:51,621 --> 00:20:54,620
When they would use these jackhammers for any length of time,

280
00:20:54,621 --> 00:20:58,620
they would actually take their shoes and put them in the form of a V,

281
00:20:58,621 --> 00:21:02,860
a V and they would use that V to get the hole started.

282
00:21:02,861 --> 00:21:06,820
And they would go through a pair of shoes by not wearing out the bottom, but actually

283
00:21:06,821 --> 00:21:10,440
wearing the insteps out first.

284
00:21:10,441 --> 00:21:16,720
Finally, pneumatic devices and hand tools were used to grind rough areas, then polish

285
00:21:16,721 --> 00:21:21,520
the finish to a smooth surface in a process called bumping.

286
00:21:21,521 --> 00:21:23,000
This is a Dalit hand facer.

287
00:21:23,001 --> 00:21:24,640
It also was run with air.

288
00:21:24,641 --> 00:21:26,920
Air would come into the back.

289
00:21:26,921 --> 00:21:32,960
The four star drill bit was placed in the dallit hand facer, but it wasn't locked in

290
00:21:32,961 --> 00:21:34,360
place.

291
00:21:34,361 --> 00:21:38,240
And the reason that he didn't lock it in place is because he didn't want it to drill a hole.

292
00:21:38,241 --> 00:21:41,140
All he wanted to do was bump the faces smooth.

293
00:21:41,141 --> 00:21:44,920
So this was used and it just went around and bumped the faces smooth just as though you

294
00:21:44,921 --> 00:21:45,920
would use sandpaper.

295
00:21:45,921 --> 00:21:50,920
He had to be pretty strong to do that all day because that's pretty heavy, especially

296
00:21:50,921 --> 00:21:55,820
for an old man like me.

297
00:21:55,821 --> 00:22:00,000
When working so close to the enormous heads, it was difficult to have a sense of the finished

298
00:22:00,001 --> 00:22:01,000
design.

299
00:22:01,001 --> 00:22:06,700
So to help the men get the feel of the sculpture, Borglum had five-foot models of each head

300
00:22:06,701 --> 00:22:09,740
placed on top of the mountain to be used as guides.

301
00:22:09,741 --> 00:22:14,360
The workers talked about how big the faces were when you were right on the faces working

302
00:22:14,361 --> 00:22:15,360
up there.

303
00:22:15,361 --> 00:22:21,800
So he decided that what he would do was create these smaller life-size models.

304
00:22:21,801 --> 00:22:25,260
Then he would take them and transfer them right up on the mountain.

305
00:22:25,261 --> 00:22:30,600
the workers could actually feel the inside of the nose and underneath the eyes and they

306
00:22:30,601 --> 00:22:34,420
can actually tell where they were working and he could point out better if you're going

307
00:22:34,421 --> 00:22:37,700
to go back this far this is what you'll be doing and this is what it'll look like when

308
00:22:37,701 --> 00:22:41,120
you're done.

309
00:22:41,121 --> 00:22:43,560
We needed jobs, that was the main thing.

310
00:22:43,561 --> 00:22:50,240
I started out at 50 cents an hour and at that time we worked six days a week and every two

311
00:22:50,241 --> 00:22:57,600
weeks we got a check for $52 and that was really good money at that time I was happy to get it.

312
00:22:57,601 --> 00:23:03,339
At that time in South Dakota unemployment was running at 34 percent.

313
00:23:03,340 --> 00:23:05,280
People needed the jobs,

314
00:23:05,281 --> 00:23:10,800
but experience in stone carving or hard rock mining were not necessary.

315
00:23:10,801 --> 00:23:16,094
Actually probably got the job because I was a good baseball player. Mr.

316
00:23:16,095 --> 00:23:17,660
Borgham's son Lincoln

317
00:23:17,661 --> 00:23:21,260
was a big baseball fan.

318
00:23:21,261 --> 00:23:24,160
So Lincoln thought, well, I'll hire baseball players

319
00:23:24,161 --> 00:23:26,740
and we'll have a baseball team up here.

320
00:23:26,741 --> 00:23:28,440
The Mount Rushmore baseball team

321
00:23:28,441 --> 00:23:31,200
became a local institution, went all the way

322
00:23:31,201 --> 00:23:34,380
to the state championship on several occasions.

323
00:23:34,381 --> 00:23:36,480
In fact, recruiting drillers and infielders

324
00:23:36,481 --> 00:23:39,440
was often done simultaneously.

325
00:23:39,441 --> 00:23:40,680
I played shortstop.

326
00:23:40,681 --> 00:23:43,080
That's the reason I got the job.

327
00:23:43,081 --> 00:23:44,760
It was not surprising that these men

328
00:23:44,761 --> 00:23:46,320
would play well together on a team.

329
00:23:46,321 --> 00:23:50,920
they had a special bond, sharing the same demanding and dangerous task.

330
00:23:51,640 --> 00:23:54,680
What is surprising is that in the 14 years of construction,

331
00:23:54,900 --> 00:23:57,140
there were no critical accidents or deaths.

332
00:23:57,920 --> 00:23:59,840
When you strapped yourself in one of those things

333
00:23:59,841 --> 00:24:02,900
and they dropped a boy down over that, he was pretty careful.

334
00:24:03,420 --> 00:24:05,740
I remember at that time, just an example,

335
00:24:06,520 --> 00:24:09,020
I smoked a pipe a lot at that time,

336
00:24:09,021 --> 00:24:13,760
and I had been silly enough to cut a hole in my respirator with a razor blade

337
00:24:13,761 --> 00:24:16,160
and had my pipe sticking through my respirator

338
00:24:16,161 --> 00:24:20,860
when Lincoln came up one time and he saw me down there.

339
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:23,540
Right away, he comes right down.

340
00:24:23,640 --> 00:24:25,200
He has a respirator in his pocket.

341
00:24:25,840 --> 00:24:29,240
Of course, I didn't have the pipe when he'd come down

342
00:24:29,241 --> 00:24:30,560
because I see him coming.

343
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:34,040
And he says, that'll be all of that.

344
00:24:34,900 --> 00:24:38,180
In contrast to his imposing father, Lincoln Borglum

345
00:24:38,181 --> 00:24:40,200
seemed a silent partner on the project,

346
00:24:40,820 --> 00:24:44,020
yet he was most responsible for supervising progress

347
00:24:44,021 --> 00:24:45,820
and employee relations.

348
00:24:46,160 --> 00:24:51,160
Mr. Borglum's son, Lincoln, he doesn't get any credit at all

349
00:24:51,260 --> 00:24:54,960
for the work he'd done, or at least very little.

350
00:24:55,060 --> 00:24:57,600
And Lincoln, he was here all the time.

351
00:24:57,700 --> 00:24:58,700
Quit college.

352
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:02,080
And he worked up there, and Mr. Borglum

353
00:25:02,180 --> 00:25:05,320
was gone a lot of times for three, four months at a time.

354
00:25:05,420 --> 00:25:12,060
And Lincoln actually supervised the work that was being done.

355
00:25:12,160 --> 00:25:15,120
And he doesn't get much credit at all.

356
00:25:15,121 --> 00:25:17,880
And I think he deserves a lot of credit.

357
00:25:17,980 --> 00:25:21,120
Gutsen was the exacting taskmaster.

358
00:25:21,220 --> 00:25:23,520
Lincoln would often have to smooth over the hurt feelings

359
00:25:23,620 --> 00:25:25,560
caused by his father.

360
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:29,860
We didn't get too much contact with Mr. Borglum.

361
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:32,300
We all knew him, spoke to him, and so forth.

362
00:25:32,400 --> 00:25:34,480
But we didn't get close to him.

363
00:25:34,580 --> 00:25:38,780
He was very quick to make decisions.

364
00:25:38,880 --> 00:25:41,380
And he wanted everything perfect.

365
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:45,120
There was a lot of people there that I know one

366
00:25:45,121 --> 00:25:48,220
of the drillers that was a finished driller,

367
00:25:48,221 --> 00:25:51,720
been fired five or six times on the weekend,

368
00:25:51,721 --> 00:25:55,860
and then Lincoln would come back and get him on Monday morning, see?

369
00:25:55,861 --> 00:25:59,060
But he was different.

370
00:25:59,061 --> 00:26:02,640
Gutzum Borglum's reputation for confrontation

371
00:26:02,641 --> 00:26:06,540
had been firmly established before the Mount Rushmore project began,

372
00:26:06,541 --> 00:26:10,680
but with the onset of the Depression and the resulting shortage of funds,

373
00:26:10,681 --> 00:26:15,120
he found it ever harder to control his temper.

374
00:26:15,121 --> 00:26:29,121
In 1929, when the stock market crashed, so did the American spirit.

375
00:26:33,620 --> 00:26:37,900
The Great Depression quickly swept across the United States, ushering in a season of gloom.

376
00:26:38,740 --> 00:26:40,320
Twelve million people were out of work.

377
00:26:40,940 --> 00:26:43,000
Bad times were of biblical proportions.

378
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:48,100
Families were torn apart as men combed the nation

379
00:26:48,101 --> 00:26:53,360
for any kind of work at any rate of pay.

380
00:26:53,361 --> 00:26:55,840
And before long, dwindling resources

381
00:26:55,841 --> 00:27:00,840
caused work to become sporadic on Borglum's Mount Rushmore.

382
00:27:00,841 --> 00:27:06,240
Gutzin could not understand how at Mount Rushmore

383
00:27:06,241 --> 00:27:09,640
anybody could tell him there isn't enough in the treasury

384
00:27:09,641 --> 00:27:11,940
for the next box of dynamite.

385
00:27:11,941 --> 00:27:17,000
He's carving stone that's going to be recognizable 100,000 years from now.

386
00:27:17,300 --> 00:27:20,220
How can you tell him today's production must stop?

387
00:27:20,960 --> 00:27:27,900
He could never understand the gift he was leaving for the world being related to dollars.

388
00:27:29,980 --> 00:27:36,120
Funding for the monument was limited to nickels and dimes donated by school children and matched by the government.

389
00:27:36,121 --> 00:27:40,120
Major federal grant money was needed at a time when none was available,

390
00:27:40,121 --> 00:27:43,120
especially for such an odd and overwhelming project.

391
00:27:43,121 --> 00:27:48,120
But ironically, what had started as a tourist attraction in the wilds of South Dakota

392
00:27:48,121 --> 00:27:51,120
was becoming an important symbol of national pride

393
00:27:51,121 --> 00:27:55,120
in a period when pride was about all people had left.

394
00:27:55,121 --> 00:27:59,120
Millions throughout the world envy us the privilege and right to call ourselves Americans.

395
00:27:59,121 --> 00:28:04,120
Let us prove our deep appreciation of that privilege by rededicating ourselves to true Americanism,

396
00:28:04,121 --> 00:28:10,180
Americanism with faith in God, faith in man, faith in liberty, and faith in America.

397
00:28:10,900 --> 00:28:16,060
The Roosevelt administration's vigorous promotion of a revitalized sense of Americanism

398
00:28:16,061 --> 00:28:19,446
inspired Borglum with a new publicity strategy.

399
00:28:19,447 --> 00:28:22,760
It was clear that his relentless dedication to

400
00:28:22,761 --> 00:28:27,460
the project and the monument's reverence toward the democratic dream were perfect springboards

401
00:28:27,461 --> 00:28:32,422
for the patriotic government's support of Mount Rushmore, and Borglum knew it.

402
00:28:32,423 --> 00:28:33,440
Using his talent

403
00:28:33,441 --> 00:28:38,760
for self-promotion, Gutsen kicked off a series of theatrical dedications to mark each stage

404
00:28:38,761 --> 00:28:44,000
of the carving process and to keep the public and political eye focused on his dream.

405
00:28:45,100 --> 00:28:49,980
America's gigantic Mount Rushmore Memorial is vested by President Roosevelt, who comes

406
00:28:49,981 --> 00:28:51,766
to dedicate the Jefferson figure.

407
00:28:51,767 --> 00:28:54,580
Sculptor Gutsen Borglum's daughter gives the signal.

408
00:28:55,960 --> 00:28:59,680
That shot was finally just above what will finally be Jefferson's hand.

409
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:09,780
Egypt's ferreals conceive nothing so spectacular as this enduring monument in South Dakota's

410
00:29:09,781 --> 00:29:14,744
Black Hills. The 65-foot Jefferson head alone is three times bigger than Sphinx.

411
00:29:14,745 --> 00:29:15,445
The four

412
00:29:15,446 --> 00:29:19,900
immense heads, Washington, Jefferson, Lincoln, and Theodore Roosevelt, when completed, will

413
00:29:19,901 --> 00:29:24,200
be the world's greatest patriotic memorial, America's heritage to future generations.

414
00:29:25,120 --> 00:29:29,680
Borglum loved a big show, but his grandest publicity scheme was destined to strain

415
00:29:29,681 --> 00:29:32,180
friendships and cost him a powerful ally.

416
00:29:40,020 --> 00:29:42,720
He loved America and wanted to proclaim it

417
00:29:42,721 --> 00:29:45,160
in letters 20 feet tall.

418
00:29:45,161 --> 00:29:46,900
His original plan for Mount Rushmore

419
00:29:46,901 --> 00:29:50,400
included a giant timeline of American history.

420
00:29:50,401 --> 00:29:52,000
He called it the entablature.

421
00:29:54,580 --> 00:29:57,800
Calvin Coolidge had been selected to write the 500 word

422
00:29:57,801 --> 00:30:02,180
composition as a condition of federal funding in the 1929 Rushmore bill which

423
00:30:02,181 --> 00:30:08,480
Coolidge himself had signed into law. In 1930 the ex-president sent a rough draft

424
00:30:08,481 --> 00:30:13,820
of the composition to South Dakota. Borglum promptly edited it to suit his

425
00:30:13,821 --> 00:30:17,420
own vision of American history, releasing it immediately to anxious

426
00:30:17,421 --> 00:30:21,860
reporters. Coolidge was shocked to see an altered version of his text published

427
00:30:21,861 --> 00:30:27,540
in newspapers. This is not what I wrote, he told Borglum, but it's what you meant

428
00:30:27,541 --> 00:30:30,869
to write, Borglum replied, and he began carving.

429
00:30:30,870 --> 00:30:34,060
The public was astounded at Borglum's audacity

430
00:30:34,061 --> 00:30:36,463
and the embarrassing tempest it inspired.

431
00:30:36,464 --> 00:30:39,160
It destroyed the friendship between the artist

432
00:30:39,161 --> 00:30:39,916
and Coolidge.

433
00:30:39,917 --> 00:30:44,220
They never spoke again, and the entablature project was halted by Congress

434
00:30:44,221 --> 00:30:48,211
until it accurately reflected Coolidge's composition.

435
00:30:48,212 --> 00:30:50,620
In 1934, after Coolidge had died

436
00:30:50,621 --> 00:30:55,080
and Congress was distracted, an ingenious newspaper man saw an opportunity to generate

437
00:30:55,081 --> 00:30:59,743
publicity and sell papers with a new twist on Borglum's entablature.

438
00:30:59,744 --> 00:31:01,320
William Randolph Hearst

439
00:31:01,321 --> 00:31:08,565
conspired with Borglum on a national promotion. Who will write the epic of America?

440
00:31:08,566 --> 00:31:09,700
Here on Mount

441
00:31:09,701 --> 00:31:14,280
Rushmore, beside the colossal figures of Washington and other leaders, will be carved an eloquent

442
00:31:14,281 --> 00:31:16,126
summary of the nation's development.

443
00:31:16,127 --> 00:31:19,100
To this, the public is invited to contribute in a gigantic

444
00:31:19,101 --> 00:31:22,374
countrywide contest sponsored by the Hearst newspapers.

445
00:31:22,375 --> 00:31:24,220
Cash and other prizes go to the

446
00:31:24,221 --> 00:31:28,660
winners whose combined text would be chiseled in the solid rock by Goodson Borglum, famous

447
00:31:28,661 --> 00:31:32,900
sculptor. A mighty and permanent salute to the spirit of progress.

448
00:31:35,620 --> 00:31:42,700
Over a million people, historians and teachers and citizens and congressmen, all were writing

449
00:31:42,701 --> 00:31:47,904
histories for this problem and finding a suitable text.

450
00:31:47,905 --> 00:31:51,500
In these rare outtakes from a newsreel

451
00:31:51,501 --> 00:31:54,660
that was never released, Borglum awarded the winning student authors.

452
00:31:55,860 --> 00:32:01,620
These three young men were awarded prizes in the successful competition,

453
00:32:01,840 --> 00:32:03,680
successful national competition.

454
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:10,120
These three young men were awarded...

455
00:32:10,121 --> 00:32:15,960
I met the challenge and I wrote it the way I thought it should be

456
00:32:15,961 --> 00:32:21,060
and I still believe what I read from others and the history of our country

457
00:32:21,061 --> 00:32:23,560
that it did the best I could at the time.

458
00:32:24,400 --> 00:32:27,960
In the end, however, Burkett's words were never carved in stone.

459
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:29,940
The idea had always been impractical.

460
00:32:30,040 --> 00:32:32,140
There wasn't enough stone for the full text.

461
00:32:32,920 --> 00:32:36,000
Borglum envisioned an entablature where the Lincoln figure is today.

462
00:32:36,900 --> 00:32:41,900
And then when he had to move the Jefferson figure over to the left of Washington,

463
00:32:42,120 --> 00:32:43,860
he also had to move the Lincoln figure.

464
00:32:44,300 --> 00:32:47,540
So that took away where the entablature would have been.

465
00:32:47,541 --> 00:32:53,920
Borglum was disappointed that the entablature fell through, but this wasn't to be the only

466
00:32:53,921 --> 00:32:57,720
unfinished dream on the mountain.

467
00:32:57,721 --> 00:33:10,780
I think that we can perhaps meditate a little on those Americans 10,000 years from now,

468
00:33:10,781 --> 00:33:18,040
the weathering of the face of Washington and Jefferson and Lincoln shall have

469
00:33:18,041 --> 00:33:26,540
proceeded to a depth of perhaps a tenth of an inch. Ten thousand years from now,

470
00:33:26,541 --> 00:33:31,060
I think we can meditate and wonder whether our descendants, because I think they'll

471
00:33:31,061 --> 00:33:38,220
still be here, what they will think about us and let us hope that at least they

472
00:33:38,221 --> 00:33:41,091
will give us the benefit of the doubt.

473
00:33:41,092 --> 00:33:45,020
That they will believe that we have honestly striven

474
00:33:45,740 --> 00:33:53,180
in our day and generation to preserve for our descendants a decent land to live in

475
00:33:53,820 --> 00:33:57,500
and a decent form of government to operate under.

476
00:33:57,501 --> 00:34:01,180
10,000 years from now the faces of Mount Rushmore

477
00:34:01,181 --> 00:34:06,060
may still be there but will anyone remember who these giants were or the people who honored them?

478
00:34:06,061 --> 00:34:11,160
Borglum tackled that problem by designing a hall of records to be drilled in the granite

479
00:34:11,161 --> 00:34:17,420
behind the heads to house copies of the Constitution, a history of the United States, busts of 24

480
00:34:17,421 --> 00:34:24,938
famous Americans. The plan was the hall would be carved behind Lincoln's head.

481
00:34:24,939 --> 00:34:26,480
There would be a

482
00:34:26,481 --> 00:34:32,660
tunnelway extending a hundred feet into the stone, leading visitors into an enormous domed room that

483
00:34:32,661 --> 00:34:35,840
would house historic artifacts.

484
00:34:35,841 --> 00:34:39,140
I would never worked in there if I'd starved to death.

485
00:34:39,141 --> 00:34:40,820
They were drilling dry.

486
00:34:40,821 --> 00:34:42,600
You couldn't recognize them when they come out

487
00:34:42,601 --> 00:34:44,420
till you blew them off.

488
00:34:44,421 --> 00:34:47,980
That dust, it was terrible.

489
00:34:47,981 --> 00:34:50,440
It was a bad situation.

490
00:34:50,441 --> 00:34:52,860
In many ways, carving the Hall of Records

491
00:34:52,861 --> 00:34:56,420
was more difficult than sculpting the portraits.

492
00:34:56,421 --> 00:34:58,580
Work on the Hall of Records ended quickly

493
00:34:58,581 --> 00:35:01,840
after Borglum's death in 1941.

494
00:35:01,841 --> 00:35:06,100
World War loomed, and Congress wanted the monument finished fast.

495
00:35:06,620 --> 00:35:09,260
I believe that Mr. Borglum was a real visionary.

496
00:35:09,960 --> 00:35:14,380
He wanted no chance that this monument to America,

497
00:35:14,381 --> 00:35:18,820
to freedom and democracy, would be lost to our future generations.

498
00:35:19,660 --> 00:35:25,200
And he wanted to ensure that we had that record in a secure environment,

499
00:35:25,460 --> 00:35:27,220
and that was to be the Hall of Records.

500
00:35:28,040 --> 00:35:34,000
The original plans were to have a tunnel way into the mountain about 100 feet

501
00:35:34,001 --> 00:35:36,580
and then a very large domed-out room.

502
00:35:37,120 --> 00:35:40,080
We have a tunnel way into the mountain of about 68 feet

503
00:35:40,081 --> 00:35:43,280
that was completed by the workers before work was halted.

504
00:35:44,140 --> 00:35:47,280
Well, I think the numbers were placed there by Gutz and Borglum

505
00:35:47,281 --> 00:35:49,860
to indicate the depth at which drilling needed to occur.

506
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:55,040
In this case, I'd say it looks like a depth of 12 inches was indicated.

507
00:35:55,041 --> 00:35:59,280
and then the blasting that would take it off to a cleaner level,

508
00:36:00,060 --> 00:36:02,140
consistent with the depth of the honeycombing.

509
00:36:02,760 --> 00:36:06,860
And then this blasted level had yet to be finally finished

510
00:36:06,861 --> 00:36:10,220
to get to the relatively smooth process we see on the faces

511
00:36:10,221 --> 00:36:11,760
or outside the Hall of Records.

512
00:36:12,720 --> 00:36:15,540
And in the Hall of Records, you can see the further in you go,

513
00:36:15,541 --> 00:36:20,640
the rougher or earlier on in that entire process that the workers were.

514
00:36:21,360 --> 00:36:23,560
Because it wasn't finished, what is preserved

515
00:36:23,561 --> 00:36:26,560
illustrates the process of construction.

516
00:36:26,561 --> 00:36:29,560
You do feel like you're very much standing

517
00:36:29,561 --> 00:36:32,560
on the doorstep of history at the Hall of Records.

518
00:36:32,561 --> 00:36:35,560
And part of the reason, I think, is because it is this

519
00:36:35,561 --> 00:36:38,560
unfinished dream concept that we see here.

520
00:36:46,560 --> 00:36:50,560
The mountain and the man, their destinies linked,

521
00:36:50,561 --> 00:36:58,320
linked spirits melded as one both imposing overstated larger than life both possessed of

522
00:36:58,321 --> 00:37:05,360
an inner grace both grand representations of the american spirit so it seemed beyond belief that one

523
00:37:05,361 --> 00:37:19,361
could exist without the other yet on march 6 1941 gutson borglum died at the age of 73.

524
00:37:20,560 --> 00:37:27,620
Without the chief to bully and boast, activity on the monument seemed to enter a state of

525
00:37:27,621 --> 00:37:33,680
suspended animation, incomplete, stuck in a moment of time between dreams and reality,

526
00:37:33,681 --> 00:37:40,320
between promise and power, its full vision still frozen under countless tons of ageless

527
00:37:40,321 --> 00:37:45,740
granite.

528
00:37:45,741 --> 00:37:50,620
At the beginning of World War II, the United States had larger issues to consider.

529
00:37:50,621 --> 00:37:55,420
Gutsen's son Lincoln was commissioned to stop the work and clean up the site.

530
00:37:55,421 --> 00:38:00,460
Tools were disassembled, Borglum's models packed away, and Lincoln climbed the peak

531
00:38:00,461 --> 00:38:06,180
one last time to say goodbye to his father's dream.

532
00:38:06,181 --> 00:38:18,900
The models which my father and I had made and changed so many times showed the figures

533
00:38:18,901 --> 00:38:21,520
were to be executed down to the waist.

534
00:38:21,521 --> 00:38:25,500
I felt they were just as effective the way they are today.

535
00:38:25,501 --> 00:38:30,180
On my final report to the commission, I stated that the granite stairway and the hall of

536
00:38:30,181 --> 00:38:33,500
records were essential to the memorial's completion.

537
00:38:33,501 --> 00:38:36,100
I felt that the memorial would become a riddle to people

538
00:38:36,101 --> 00:38:40,860
a few thousand years from now without explanatory data.

539
00:38:40,861 --> 00:38:44,260
Today, the official view is that the memorial is finished,

540
00:38:44,261 --> 00:38:47,360
and there are definitely no plans for more carving.

541
00:38:47,361 --> 00:38:51,940
Actually, there is no stone on Rushmore for another face.

542
00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:56,220
I do feel that the great symbolic message of the memorial

543
00:38:56,221 --> 00:38:58,880
is there.

544
00:38:58,881 --> 00:39:00,740
Many people wonder about should Mount Rushmore ever

545
00:39:00,741 --> 00:39:01,640
be completed.

546
00:39:01,641 --> 00:39:03,600
I think the answer to that is no.

547
00:39:03,601 --> 00:39:05,740
Certainly more work could have been done on it.

548
00:39:05,741 --> 00:39:08,540
But there's a story to be told in unfinished dreams,

549
00:39:08,541 --> 00:39:10,160
as well as finished dreams.

550
00:39:10,161 --> 00:39:11,700
It's a piece of art in its own right.

551
00:39:11,701 --> 00:39:14,480
You know, it's one man's conception of the birth and growth

552
00:39:14,481 --> 00:39:15,700
of our country.

553
00:39:15,701 --> 00:39:17,580
And what these four presidents represented

554
00:39:17,581 --> 00:39:20,220
to the first 150 years of our country,

555
00:39:20,221 --> 00:39:22,680
and changing or adding a head to Mount Rushmore now

556
00:39:22,681 --> 00:39:24,280
would be tantamount to, if you will,

557
00:39:24,281 --> 00:39:27,480
changing the smile on the Mona Lisa.

558
00:39:27,481 --> 00:39:30,360
As a man grows old, so does a mountain.

559
00:39:30,361 --> 00:39:37,340
The Black Hills themselves are old men, granite stubs left from the erosion of stone giants tens of millions of years ago.

560
00:39:37,780 --> 00:39:39,700
Their erosion continues today.

561
00:39:40,340 --> 00:39:43,760
The granite in the Black Hills is full of cracks.

562
00:39:44,360 --> 00:39:46,720
And the mountain itself has dozens.

563
00:39:46,920 --> 00:39:48,320
This is a natural crack.

564
00:39:48,760 --> 00:39:53,540
The primary thing we would do is to try to keep the moisture out of them.

565
00:39:53,541 --> 00:39:57,540
because the biggest concern we'd have is moisture getting in,

566
00:39:57,640 --> 00:40:00,920
freezing and thawing cycles, and an actual wedging process

567
00:40:00,921 --> 00:40:04,060
whereby over many hundreds or thousands of years

568
00:40:04,061 --> 00:40:06,780
that wedging process could cause some problems.

569
00:40:07,600 --> 00:40:11,040
While we know there's no imminent danger for the mountain itself,

570
00:40:11,140 --> 00:40:13,000
we do know that there's at least three cracks

571
00:40:13,001 --> 00:40:15,780
that pose future problems in the stability of the mountain.

572
00:40:16,120 --> 00:40:18,480
One of those cracks is in Lincoln's left eye,

573
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:20,500
another is in Roosevelt's hairline,

574
00:40:20,501 --> 00:40:22,700
and another is in Washington's right ear.

575
00:40:23,540 --> 00:40:36,460
This crack runs all the way through the left side of Washington's head and intersects with

576
00:40:36,461 --> 00:40:39,650
another crack coming in on a lateral plane.

577
00:40:39,651 --> 00:40:42,840
So we have a huge block of granite and it's

578
00:40:42,841 --> 00:40:45,772
not going anywhere. It weighs many, many tons.

579
00:40:45,773 --> 00:40:48,640
We've been measuring the spread of this crack

580
00:40:48,641 --> 00:40:51,460
with three pins that are placed.

581
00:40:51,461 --> 00:40:55,400
And about every month or so, somebody from our staff

582
00:40:55,401 --> 00:40:57,680
takes a measurement for the last 10 years.

583
00:40:57,681 --> 00:41:01,000
And we have had no detectable spread on this crack.

584
00:41:01,001 --> 00:41:03,260
The material that's patching this crack

585
00:41:03,261 --> 00:41:06,520
is the mixture that Gutz and Borglum used, which

586
00:41:06,521 --> 00:41:10,060
is the mixture of white lead, granite dust, and linseed oil.

587
00:41:10,061 --> 00:41:13,080
The substance that we've patched these cracks with most

588
00:41:13,081 --> 00:41:17,740
recently is a silicone-based, somewhat pliable substance

589
00:41:17,741 --> 00:41:22,500
that can expand and contract at this rate with the rock.

590
00:41:26,580 --> 00:41:31,100
The eerie silence of the Black Hills draws seekers still

591
00:41:31,101 --> 00:41:34,840
and raises questions that can't be answered.

592
00:41:34,841 --> 00:41:37,920
Is there such a thing as eternity?

593
00:41:37,921 --> 00:41:40,620
Or is forever a moment we hold in our hearts?

594
00:41:47,740 --> 00:42:01,740
Cary, in 1933, when I was a little girl, we used to come up here.

595
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:09,520
I lived in Hot Springs, and I'd come up here on Sundays or during the week, and Borglum

596
00:42:09,521 --> 00:42:12,460
would talk to us, and we watched Borglum carve those.

597
00:42:13,060 --> 00:42:17,720
There used to be stairs that came in behind Abraham Lincoln, and you could

598
00:42:17,721 --> 00:42:18,920
could walk up through there.

599
00:42:18,921 --> 00:42:21,920
They weren't restricted like they are today.

600
00:42:21,921 --> 00:42:23,820
And you could get up on top of there

601
00:42:23,821 --> 00:42:25,320
when they weren't working.

602
00:42:25,321 --> 00:42:30,120
And it's so amazing to me that anybody could actually

603
00:42:30,121 --> 00:42:32,480
carry out a dream like that.

604
00:42:32,481 --> 00:42:33,920
The Mount Rushmore Visitor Center

605
00:42:33,921 --> 00:42:37,160
welcomes over 2 and 1 half million tourists a year,

606
00:42:37,161 --> 00:42:40,200
some reliving a childhood experience,

607
00:42:40,201 --> 00:42:42,120
others on a patriotic pilgrimage.

608
00:42:44,780 --> 00:42:47,200
As the public comes to the memorial

609
00:42:47,201 --> 00:42:50,503
they walk out on the terrace for the first time if they've never seen it.

610
00:42:50,504 --> 00:42:51,680
I think they first ask how

611
00:42:51,681 --> 00:42:56,800
it was done, but after they've stood there a while and thought about how, I think then they start to

612
00:42:56,801 --> 00:42:57,718
reflect on why.

613
00:42:57,719 --> 00:43:03,040
And the why is very important because it explains America and what makes America great.

614
00:43:04,720 --> 00:43:10,199
Mount Rushmore is the work of a dreamer inspired by a nation of dreamers.

615
00:43:10,200 --> 00:43:12,000
It was one man's vision,

616
00:43:12,001 --> 00:43:16,100
but it demanded the sweat and sacrifice of many ordinary men and women

617
00:43:16,101 --> 00:43:18,300
who would chisel their history in stone

618
00:43:18,301 --> 00:43:21,440
and celebrate the promise of the land they loved.

619
00:43:21,860 --> 00:43:26,180
There was a group of people that turned to be a family that worked up here,

620
00:43:26,940 --> 00:43:28,560
and that was always amazing to me.

621
00:43:28,660 --> 00:43:30,140
They never got much credit.

622
00:43:30,840 --> 00:43:32,300
They got very little credit.

623
00:43:32,640 --> 00:43:35,920
When they took a picture of them, nobody even took time to name them.

624
00:43:36,780 --> 00:43:39,160
But I'll tell you, I would like to see them named,

625
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:40,680
and so would some of their widows.

626
00:43:40,681 --> 00:43:45,680
Those old ones that died from working up here all those years,

627
00:43:45,681 --> 00:43:47,680
with the dust in their lungs and that,

628
00:43:47,681 --> 00:43:49,680
somebody ought to name them.

629
00:45:10,680 --> 00:45:12,680
You

